<!--
    A byte-masked compact jump table that is its function's last, followed
    by the table of a function whose index a csel/csinc chain selects;
    assembled from
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/switchnextcsel_aarch64.s
    (the .text and .rodata of switchnextcsel_aarch64).

    `name_of` indexes tab_names (3 bytes at 0x11084) with `x0 & 0xff`, so
    the basic model reads 256 rows. The rows past its three are pick's table
    (0x11087) and the bytes after it, measured from name_of's base: 1 and 3
    land inside name_of, 13 in pick, 0 on name_of's first case, so the
    run-on cut keeps every row and switchextent ends the table at the next
    function's table. pick's index is
        mov w9, #4; mov w10, #2; cmp w0, #3; csel w9, w10, w9, lo
        cmp w1, #0xff; csinc w8, w9, wzr, hs; cmp w2, #7; csel w8, wzr, w8, lo
    and pick adds it to the table's address before the load
    (`add x9, x9, x8; ldrb w11, [x9]`). Each csel/csinc lifts to a branch
    inside the instruction; the linear decode that names pick's table must
    not take the last copy before it as the value, or it reads the load as
    one from a constant address, finds no table in pick, and name_of keeps
    phantom cases running its own case bodies' second instructions and
    pick's code.
-->
